.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
One stretch of text with a look of its own\&. 

A label is one face in one colour\&. A span says that some run of bytes in it is not: that a phrase is bold, that a term is set in the code face, that a word carries a background or a line through it\&.
.PP
Spans describe the text rather than act on it, so the order they are given in does not matter and a byte covered by no span is drawn the way the widget's style says\&. A field left at zero means the same thing: take the widget's\&. That is what makes the common case short, because a span marking one word bold sets \fCstart\fP, \fCend\fP and \fCbold\fP and nothing else\&.
.PP
Bold and italic are wishes rather than faces\&. A program cannot name the bold handle for whatever a phrase happens to be set in, so it asks, and the family answers; see schultz_font_at_style\&. Setting \fCfont\fP names a face outright, and bold and italic are then resolved within that face's family\&. 
.PP
